        setup: {
          players: '4 players in two fixed partnerships (A+C vs B+D).',
          deck: '24 cards: Ace (low) through Queen (high) of two suits (e.g., Red and Black).',
          equipment: 'Standard deck and privacy screens for card arrangement.',
          dealing: 'Each player is dealt 6 cards face-down. They arrange their cards in front of them in **ascending order of value** from left to right.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Be the first player to correctly declare the identities of all cards still in play.',
          gameplay: "1. **The Show**: Your partner privately shows you one of their face-down cards. You now know its identity and position. 2. **The Guess**: You must guess the identity of one of your OPPONENTS' face-down cards (Position + Rank). 3. **The Reveal**: If correct: The opponent turns the card face-up permanently. If incorrect: YOU must choose one of your own face-down cards to reveal face-up. 4. **Winning Declaration**: A player can 'Declare' at any time. They must correctly identify EVERY remaining face-down card in the game (teammates and opponents). If they get even one wrong, their team loses immediately.",
          keyRules: [
            'Cards arranged in ascending order left to right.',
            'Partner shows you one card (identity and position).',
            "Guess opponent's card by position and rank.",
            'Correct guess: opponent reveals that card.',
            'Incorrect guess: you must reveal one of your own cards.',
            'Can declare at any time to name all remaining face-down cards.',
            'Declaration must be 100% correct or your team loses.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Use the ascending order to your advantage. If an opponent reveals a 10 at Position 4 (out of 6), you know that Positions 1-3 MUST be lower than 10, and Positions 5-6 MUST be higher than 10.',
          intermediate: "The Partner Signal: When your partner shows you a card, they are often showing you a 'boundary' card (like a 6 or 7). Use this to deduce their other cards and narrow down the possibilities for the opponents.",
          advanced: "Elimination Logic: If you know your team has both Aces, and no opponent has revealed an Ace yet, you can 'waste' a guess on an opponent's first position to see if they reveal a 2 or 3. Even an incorrect guess on your part provides information to your partner.",
          tips: [
            'Always track which ranks have been fully revealed (both suits). Once both Queens are face-up, they are out of the deck possibilities.',
            "Be careful with your 'Self-Reveal' when you guess wrong. Reveal cards that you think your partner already deduced to give the opponents the least amount of new info.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
